UID:36076-1794582000-1794589200@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Ethics and Political Philosophy Interest Group Talk (Selim Berker\, Harvard University)
DESCRIPTION:The Ethics and Political Philosophy Interest Group is pleased to welcome as its guest speaker Selim Berker\, the Alford Professor of Natural Religion\, Moral Philosophy\, and Civil Polity in the Department of Philosophy at Harvard University. He works on the foundations of normativity in all of its guises\, including the normative assessment of action (in ethics)\, of belief (in epistemology)\, and—increasingly—of emotion (in moral psychology and aesthetics). He is also interested in more general issues in metaphysics connected to grounding\, explanation\, and dependence\, often—but not always—with an eye toward their application in normative contexts. \nTalk Title\nOn the Metaphysics of Voting \nTalk Abstract\nDiscussions of the ethics of voting often proceed by making metaphysical assumptions. It is widely assumed that votes determine electoral outcomes by causing them\, and that an individual vote plays no role in determining an election’s winner when the margin of victory is greater than one. However\, both of these assumptions are false. Our votes determine who wins an election by metaphysically grounding that result\, not causing it. Moreover\, joint grounding does not involve individual contributions adding up to an effect\, and overdetermination via grounding does not make each full ground any less responsible for what it brings about. These observations motivate a picture on which our individual votes can be metaphysically responsible for a candidate’s victory\, no matter the final tally—and hence we can be morally responsible for casting those votes\, even in a landslide.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/ethics-and-political-philosophy-interest-group-talk-selim-berker-harvard-university/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building\, Room 418\, 170 St. George Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5R 2M8\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/berker_1_1-cropped.png
END:VEVENT
