UID:36138-1795773600-1795780800@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Early Modern Philosophy Research Interest Group (Raphaelle Dupont\, U of T)
DESCRIPTION:The Early Modern Philosophy Research Interest Group welcomes Raphaelle Dupont\, a PhD student in the department. Her main research interests are in the history of philosophy\, especially in the transition between late medieval and early modern metaphysics. Before joining the department\, she completed her Master’s studies with a thesis on Spinoza’s metaphysics. \nTalk Title\nThe Structure of Spinoza’s Parallelism \nTalk Abstract\nWhere Descartes arguably failed to explain how mind and body could interact\, Spinoza does away with the problem entirely: for him\, the two do not interact at all\, but rather evolve in complete harmony\, each within their respective separate realms or attributes. This Spinozistic doctrine of parallelism is standardly understood as running uniformly across all of the infinitely attributes of God (Thought\, Extension\, and infinitely many unknown attributes)\, yielding isomorphic causal chains in each of them (SP). Dissenters generally accept this standard view\, and only insist on adding a different\, second kind of ‘representational’ parallelism (RP) to this picture\, which is restricted to Thought and the attribute it represents. This paper interrogates both interpretations\, to argue that only RP should be preserved\, and SP abandoned. The result is a reading of Spinoza’s parallelism that is both textually faithful and resolves apparent conflicts with some of his key principles.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/early-modern-philosophy-research-interest-group-raphaelle-dupont-u-of-t/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building\, Room 418\, 170 St. George Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5R 2M8\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/Raphaelle-Dupont-resized.png
ORGANIZER;CN="Tarek Dika":MAILTO:tarek.dika@utoronto.ca
END:VEVENT
