UID:35763-1795791600-1795798800@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Global Philosophy Research Group (Monica Z. X. Ding\, U of T)
DESCRIPTION:The Global Philosophy Research Interest Group welcomes guest speaker Monica Z. X. Ding\, a postdoctoral fellow in the philosophy department at the University of Toronto. Her research focuses on the application of Classical Chinese philosophy to AI ethics. She is also interested in the nature of understanding and its applications to AI and misinformation\, as well as the use of epistemic verbs in English\, Cantonese\, and Mandarin. \nTalk Title\nCan “Correcting the Names” Help LLMs Become Morally Diverse? \nTalk Abstract\nGenuine AI moral diversity may be achievable\, not through expanding the pool or the diversity of moral beliefs in the training data\, but through structural changes to the internal mechanism of LLMs. We aim to frame the issue using Xunzian insight regarding “correcting names”. For Xunzi\, a specific moral system categorises objects into a normative taxonomy with a corresponding linguistic system of names. Linguistic systems are embodiments of moral systems. Based on Xunzi’s theory of names\, we argue that the current LLM mechanism\, such as contextual correlation prediction\, is able to respect and reproduce the normative relationship between names to a very limited degree\, and any further enhancement would require a critical change to the structure of the training methodologies. Such changes would allow LLMs to become better at correlating terms with the taxonomic systems they are embedded in\, thus not only allowing LLMs to have better performance in moral tasks in general but also enabling them to operate in distinct moral systems (such as cultural linguistic systems\, domain-specific systems)\, thus enabling these models to perform better in morally diverse contexts. The work is an attempt to include Classical Chinese philosophy into current debates on language and moral diversity\, and apply it to real-life problem\, such as AI ethics.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/global-philosophy-research-group-monica-z-x-ding-u-of-t/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building\, Room 418\, 170 St. George Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5R 2M8\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/Monica-Ding-Postdoc-resized.png
END:VEVENT
